<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dumnorix</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/54-bce">54 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dumnorix was a nobleman of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diviciacus-suessiones">Diviciacus</a>. He led the anti-Roman party among his people, married a Helvetian noblewoman and helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> in their migration of 58 BCE. In 54 BCE he refused to join Caesar's expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and was killed by Caesar's cavalry while trying to escape.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dumnorix.jpg" alt="Dumnorix" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urban · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bibracte_aristocrate_gaulois.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dumnorix, whose name is spelled Dubnoreix on coins, was the younger brother of Divitiacus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> druid and statesman. The Aedui were allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the two brothers stood on opposite sides of that alliance: Divitiacus led the pro-Roman faction and would later aid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in his conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, while Dumnorix led those who, in Caesar's words, thought it better to be dominated by their fellow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> than by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Both brothers appear in Caesar's Commentaries on the Gallic War, the main account of his life.</p><p>His name carries the Gaulish suffix -rix, related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rex and indicating kingship or rule, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambiorix">Ambiorix</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orgetorix">Orgetorix</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a>. Dumno- is related to the Gaelic domhan, world, so the name may be translated as king of the world.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Before Caesar's governorship of Transalpine Gaul, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orgetorix">Orgetorix</a> conspired with Dumnorix and Casticus of the Sequani to make themselves kings in their own tribes and then rule all of Gaul by their combined power. Orgetorix married his daughter to Dumnorix to seal the alliance, but the plot was discovered and Orgetorix died soon after amid rumours of suicide.</p><p>Dumnorix then used his influence to persuade the Sequani to let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetii">Helvetii</a> migrate through their territory. When Caesar arrived in 58 BCE and opposed the migration, he required the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a> to supply his soldiers with grain. Dumnorix, popular and followed by a large body of personal retainers, delayed the supply. His retainers formed a sizeable part of the Gallic cavalry screening Caesar's pursuit of the Helvetii, and at a key moment in one engagement he withdrew his unit, handing the Helvetii the victory.</p><p>Liscus, the chief magistrate or Vergobretus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, revealed to Caesar that Dumnorix was behind both the withheld grain and the cavalry's flight. Divitiacus interceded for his brother, and Caesar placed Dumnorix under surveillance instead of punishing him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Caesar chose Dumnorix as one of the Gaulish leaders he would take as hostages on his second expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, fearing trouble in his absence. Dumnorix pleaded fear of the sea and religious obligations, then claimed that Caesar meant to have them all killed out of sight of their people. Finally he fled the camp with the Aeduan cavalry. Pursued by the rest of Caesar's horsemen, he was killed shouting that he was a free man and a citizen of a free state, and the Aeduan cavalry returned to Caesar's service.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dumnorix” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The druid Diviciacus, who had pleaded the Aeduan cause before the Roman Senate, led the pro-Roman party, while his brother <b>Dumnorix</b> led the anti-Roman party.</blockquote><a href="/state/aedui">Aedui</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=-64" aria-label="Gaul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4618 529 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dumnorix"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5068 799h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5068 799h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=46.5,2.9&amp;year=-64">What was here in 64 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
